Recent work has endeavored to predict discourse markers (DMs) between sentence pairs using neural models, often leveraging contextual embedding models like BERT trained on large corpora of labeled sentence pairs. Despite these models achieving high accuracy on specific tasks, they remain difficult to train for new discourse marker sets or language pairs, as this typically demands retraining on massive corpora containing the new DM sets. In this paper, we propose a framework to predict unseen DM sets and translate known DMs between sets within a specific corpus or across sets in a universal set, utilizing only contextual embeddings. We assessed this approach on existing datasets, including the Multi-Genre Document级 classification dataset and the Universal Discourse Representation Theory language list. The findings indicate that our method effectively predicts unseen sets and facilitates the translation of known DM sets and the URGT language list. Consequently, contextual contextualization can be employed to predict discourse markers sets, replace explicit ones, and enable their translation between sets and languages without requiring large corpora or modifications to neural models. The code is available at \url{https://github.com/AbdulrahmanAlshammari/DMC}. The main contributions of this work are: (1) a method for predicting discourse markers sets, (2) predicting unseen sets within a corpus, (3) translating known sets between different sets, and (4) enabling the prediction of URG language markers. These contributions are detailed in Sections (2), (3), (4), and (5) respectively.

